<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William II of Holland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1227">1227</a>–<a href="/years/1256">1256</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as William II, Count of Holland</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William II of Holland was Count of Holland from 1234 and was elected King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 1247 by princes opposed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> emperor Frederick II. He began building the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binnenhof">Binnenhof</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> as his residence. He was killed in 1256 while campaigning against the West Frisians, before he could be crowned emperor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-holland.jpg" alt="William II of Holland" width="320" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Titus van der Laars (born 1861 - died 1939) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_William_II_of_Holland,_King_of_the_Holy_Roman_Empire_(SVG).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William was the eldest son and heir of Count Floris IV of Holland and Matilda of Brabant. His father was killed at a tournament at Corbie when William was only seven years old. His paternal uncles William and Otto, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Utrecht, were his guardians until 1239. He was Count of Holland and Zeeland from 1234 until his death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>With the help of his maternal uncle Duke Henry II of Brabant and Konrad von Hochstaden, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, William was elected king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iv">Pope Innocent IV</a> excommunicated Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>. He succeeded Landgrave Henry Raspe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, who had died within a year of his own election as anti-king. William extended his father's hunting residence into a palace worthy of his new status, later called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binnenhof">Binnenhof</a>, which began the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>. He then besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> for six months before capturing it from Frederick's followers, and only then could Archbishop Konrad crown him king.</p><p>On 25 January 1252 he married Elizabeth, daughter of the Welf duke Otto of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>, which brought him a degree of theoretical support from some German princes, and on 25 March 1252 he was elected King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> a second time, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>. His power, however, never extended beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>. In Holland he fought Countess Margaret II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> for control of Zeeland, and defeated the Flemish army at Westkapelle in July 1253. He granted city rights to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haarlem">Haarlem</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delft">Delft</a>, 's-Gravenzande and Alkmaar, and from 1254 fought several wars against the West Frisians, for which he built castles at Heemskerk and Haarlem.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 28 January 1256, in a battle near Hoogwoud, William was lost and tried to cross a frozen lake alone. His horse fell through the ice, and the Frisians killed him and buried him in secret under the floor of a house. His son Floris V recovered the body 26 years later, took terrible vengeance on the West Frisians, and had his father buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middelburg">Middelburg</a>. Contemporary sources, including the chronicle of Melis Stoke, portray William as an Arthurian hero. A golden statue of him stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binnenhof">Binnenhof</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>, the inner court of the parliamentary complex of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William II of Holland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dirk III defeated an imperial army at Vlaardingen in 1018; <b>William II</b> was elected King of the Romans in 1247 and began the Binnenhof at The Hague.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-holland">County of Holland</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Hoogwoud, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.72,4.94&amp;year=1245" aria-label="Hoogwoud on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of William II of Holland"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5255 5612h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5255 5612h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=52.72,4.94&amp;year=1245">What was here in 1245? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
